-0.0113983154296875, -0.02423095703125, 0.005840301513671875, 0.0052947998046875, -0.0184173583984375, 0.0256195068359375, 0.036529541015625, 0.031951904296875, -0.0212554931640625, 0.01617431640625, -0.0239105224609375, 0.002567291259765625, -0.042755126953125, -0.006805419921875, -0.0027179718017578125, -0.015655517578125, 0.02313232421875, 0.01171875, -0.0085906982421875, 0.021270751953125, -0.00846099853515625, -0.00879669189453125, -0.00872039794921875, 0.037841796875, 0.0080108642578125, -0.0161895751953125, 0.002910614013671875, 0.020172119140625, 0.0283203125, -0.0202789306640625, 0.043426513671875, -0.012603759765625, 0.0147705078125, -0.02972412109375, -0.02325439453125, 0.0306396484375, -0.023956298828125, -0.00186920166015625, -0.0253448486328125, 0.01413726806640625, 0.0256500244140625, 0.0215606689453125, -0.01837158203125, -3.534555435180664e-05, 0.01216888427734375, 0.006313323974609375, 0.0203094482421875, -0.00921630859375, 0.014251708984375, 0.048553466796875, -0.0282745361328125, -0.02740478515625, -0.016571044921875, 0.0124664306640625, 0.0289764404296875, 0.0026569366455078125, 0.0182037353515625, 0.0298309326171875, -0.019378662109375, 0.01125335693359375, -0.0075531005859375, -0.03216552734375, -0.01556396484375, 0.0350341796875, -0.00034999847412109375, -0.034576416015625, 0.0244903564453125, 0.04180908203125, -0.013031005859375, 0.01534271240234375, 0.046295166015625, -0.0333251953125, 0.01776123046875, -0.00283050537109375, -0.01233673095703125, 0.043670654296875, 0.02142333984375, -0.019989013671875, 0.02203369140625, -0.036163330078125, -0.00046443939208984375, -0.0201568603515625, -0.009796142578125, -0.0009145736694335938, 0.04443359375, -0.0043182373046875, -0.003734588623046875, 0.0281982421875, -0.045654296875, 0.0168914794921875, 0.013916015625, 0.00946044921875, -0.0029468536376953125, -0.0024280548095703125, -0.0143890380859375, 0.005828857421875, -0.002941131591796875, 0.04290771484375, -0.037017822265625, -0.0227508544921875, 0.025360107421875, 0.03778076171875, 0.031494140625, 0.033416748046875, 0.00897216796875, -0.0310211181640625, -0.0311279296875, -0.0096282958984375, -0.01103973388671875, -0.0014696121215820312, 0.045654296875, 0.037506103515625, 0.0191192626953125, 0.0070037841796875, 0.0135650634765625, 0.042388916015625, 0.015838623046875, -0.049468994140625, 0.0028667449951171875, 0.00015366077423095703, 0.0282135009765625, 0.0003695487976074219, -0.01446533203125, 0.029571533203125, -0.0106201171875, -0.04052734375, 0.019927978515625, 0.0069580078125, -0.0032672882080078125, 0.007480621337890625, -0.0143890380859375, -0.006946563720703125, -0.0071563720703125, -0.01488494873046875, -0.0198974609375, 0.0200653076171875, -0.003246307373046875, 0.039337158203125, 0.0137176513671875, -0.020843505859375, -0.0164794921875, 0.0064239501953125, 0.021820068359375, -0.017547607421875, 0.009063720703125, 0.013031005859375, -0.0198211669921875, 0.017486572265625, -0.01529693603515625, 0.001117706298828125, 0.0316162109375, -0.01428985595703125, -0.0162506103515625, 0.0279693603515625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent research has begun to investigate the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) for modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, particularly in advanced engineering contexts such as multi-phase electrical circuits, robotics, and electromagnetic systems. For example, Hitzer [1] applied GA to circuit variables and mesh analysis; Hitzer [2] extended linear systems and control theory to Clifford (geometric) algebras but mostly addressed signals homogeneous in grade; and Li & Klose [3] developed multivector approaches to engineering design. Geometric Algebra is a mathematical framework constructed on a real vector space of dimension \\( n = p+q \\) with a metric of signature \\((p, q)\\): \\(p\\) basis vectors square to \\(+1\\), \\(q\\) to \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### Notation and Preliminaries\n\nLet \\( \\mathcal{G}_{p, q} \\) be the real geometric algebra of signature \\((p, q)\\) and dimension \\(2^n\\). The standard basis elements \\(\\{\\mathbf{e}_1, \\ldots, \\mathbf{e}_n\\}\\) satisfy \\(\\mathbf{e}_i^2 = +1\\) for \\(i \\leq p\\), \\(\\mathbf{e}_i^2 = -1\\) for \\(i > p\\). A *blade* is the wedge (exterior) product of \\(r\\) orthonormal basis vectors and has *grade* \\(r\\). A general element\u2014*multivector*\u2014is a linear combination of blades of various grades. For \\(M \\in \\mathcal{G}_{p,q}\\), \\(\\langle M \\rangle_r\\) denotes the projection to its grade-\\(r\\) part (sum of all grade-\\(r\\) blades in \\(M\\)). Define the induced norm as:\n\\[\n\\| M \\| := \\left( \\sum_{r=0}^n \\| \\langle M \\rangle_r \\|_2^2 \\right)^{1/2},\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\| \\cdot \\|_2 \\) is the standard Euclidean norm for real vectors within each grade-\\(r\\) subspace.\n\nWe consider multivector-valued functions \\( M(t): [0, \\infty) \\to \\mathcal{G}_{p, q} \\), assumed piecewise continuous and absolutely integrable (\\(\\int_0^T \\|M(t)\\|\\,dt < \\infty\\) for all \\(T > 0\\)).\n\n---\n\n### Motivation\n\nThe Laplace transform is a cornerstone of systems and control engineering: it translates time-domain differential equations into frequency-domain algebraic equations, enabling the analysis of stability, resonance, feedback, and control synthesis. However, many modern applications (e.g., the analysis of a three-phase power converter using a unified geometric algebra representation that simultaneously models phase currents and spatial flux linkages as multivectors) require a frequency-domain approach for mixed-grade, GA-valued signals. Existing Laplace transforms cannot directly process such signals: engineers are forced to split signals into scalar/vector/bivector components and analyze them separately, losing geometric clarity and complicating inter-component coupling analysis. For instance, transforming a doubly-fed machine model that tracks both stator (vector) and space phasor (bivector) variables requires a unified treatment to capture all couplings, which prior scalar or even quaternion/hypercomplex Laplace transforms cannot provide.\n\n---\n\n#### Table 1: Laplace Transform Capabilities in Different Algebras\n\n| Algebraic Setting      | Scalar   | Complex   | Quaternionic  | Clifford/GA (grade-homogeneous) | GA (mixed-grade, proposed) |\n|-----------------------|----------|-----------|--------------|------------------------------|-----------------------------|\n| Noncommutativity      | No       | No        | Yes          | Yes (often ignored)           | Yes (fully handled)         |\n| Mixed-Grade Signals   | N/A      | N/A       | No           | No                            | Yes                         |\n| Full System Couplings | No       | No        | Limited      | Limited                       | Yes                         |\n| Laplace Transform     | Standard | Standard  | Special Cases| Partial (restricted grades)    | Generalized (this work)     |\n\n---\n\n### Literature Review\n\nEfforts to extend the Laplace transform beyond scalar signals include:\n\n- **Hitzer [1]:** Applies GA to circuit analysis (mesh, node), but only for static or grade-homogeneous cases. No unified operational calculus for general dynamic, mixed-grade signals is proposed.\n- **Hitzer [2]:** Extends systems and control theory to Clifford-valued settings but treats only grade-homogeneous signals, leaving mixed-grade and full noncommutative scenarios out.\n- **Li & Klose [3]:** Employ multivector approaches in engineering design, but focus on static relations and geometric synthesis; dynamic transforms and frequency-domain analysis are not addressed.\n- **B\u00fclow & Sommer [4]:** Extend Laplace transforms to quaternionic signals. However, quaternion noncommutativity leads to order ambiguities and only allows transforms in some cases, excluding general GA-valued, mixed-grade settings.\n- **Brackx et al. [5]:** Investigate Laplace/Fourier transforms on commutative hypercomplex algebras (e.g., split-complex), but their techniques fail for noncommutative GAs and do not address arbitrary grade mixtures.\n\n**Distinct Contribution:**  \nUnlike prior work, this framework gives\u2014for the first time\u2014a general, explicit, and invertible Laplace transform for arbitrary multivector-valued functions (combining all grades) in \\(\\mathcal{G}_{p,q}\\). It addresses not only the mixed-grade but also the intrinsic noncommutative structure of the GA, providing a rigorous foundation for frequency-domain system analysis with full physical coupling.\n\n---\n\n### Research Gap and Objectives\n\n**Research Gap:**  \nNo comprehensive Laplace transform exists for arbitrary multivector-valued, potentially mixed-grade and noncommuting signals, as encountered in realistic models employing GA. This precludes direct and unified frequency-domain analysis for complex systems.\n\n**Objectives:**  \n1. Formally define the Laplace transform for arbitrary multivector-valued functions \\( M(t): \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\).\n2. Demonstrate that key Laplace properties (linearity, differentiation, invertibility) hold, grade by grade, in the general (noncommutative, mixed-grade) GA setting.\n3. Illustrate application to practical engineering or physical models previously inaccessible to conventional Laplace or hypercomplex methods.\n\n**Scope Restriction and Scalability:**  \nTo keep computations tractable, we provide explicit examples up to \\( n \\leq 5 \\) (so GA dimension up to 32). For higher dimensions (practical in, e.g., spacetime or higher-dimensional robotics), direct computation becomes unwieldy; here, symbolic algebra systems (e.g., GA packages for Mathematica, Maple, or Python) or parallel algorithmic approaches could be employed to manage gradewise operations and convolutions. Advances in symbolic computation are making frequency-domain analysis of general multivector signals increasingly feasible at higher dimensions.\n\n---\n\n### Technical Background\n\n#### Multivector Functions\n\nAs above, \\(M(t)\\) is a multivector-valued function over time, written as \\(M(t) = \\sum_{r=0}^n \\langle M(t) \\rangle_r\\), where each \\(\\langle M(t) \\rangle_r\\) is the grade-\\(r\\) component.\n\n---\n\n### Laplace Transform for Multivector-Valued Functions\n\n**Definition:**  \nFor \\( M(t) = \\sum_{r=0}^n \\langle M(t) \\rangle_r \\), the Laplace transform is defined by\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\{M\\}(s) := \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} M(t)\\,dt = \\sum_{r=0}^n \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} \\langle M(t) \\rangle_r \\,dt,\n\\]\nwhere \\( e^{-st} \\) is real scalar and commutes with all GA elements. Thus, the transform separates grade-wise: the Laplace transform of \\(M\\) is the sum of the transforms of its grade components.\n\n**Invertibility:**  \nSince each grade component is integrated just as a scalar-valued function, the classical Laplace inversion formula carries over, applied to each \\(\\langle M(t)\\rangle_r\\). The noncommutative GA product plays no role in the linear Laplace operation, provided no multiplication by non-scalar functions of \\(t\\) is present within the integrand.\n\n---\n\n### Implications and Operational Issues from Noncommutativity\n\nWhile scalar Laplace transforms permit arbitrary reordering of factors, GA's noncommutativity introduces operational subtleties when dealing with system interconnections, convolutions, or transfer functions. Specifically, while the Laplace transform itself is linear and acts grade-wise (and thus unaffected by products internal to \\(M\\)), care must be taken if considering multiplication of transforms:\n- **Right/Left Multiplication:** For two multivector Laplace transforms \\(A(s), B(s)\\), their product \\(A(s)B(s)\\) depends on the order due to noncommutativity.\n- **Convolution:** For system responses involving convolution with operator kernels (e.g., a multivector impulse response), the convolution theorem requires attention: \\( \\mathcal{L}\\{M \\ast N\\}(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{M\\}(s) \\mathcal{L}\\{N\\}("}}